<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Senussi Order</h1><p class="st-meta">1843 CE – 1951 CE (approx.) · capital Jaghbub</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sufi brotherhood that built a network of desert lodges across the eastern Sahara, led resistance to France and Italy and gave Libya its first king.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ali-al-senussi">Muhammad ibn Ali al-Senussi</a>, an Algerian-born scholar, founded his brotherhood in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1837 and established its first lodge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, at al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayda-libya">Bayda</a>, in 1843. The order moved its headquarters to the remote oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaghbub">Jaghbub</a> in 1856 and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufra">Kufra</a>, beyond the reach of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tripolitania-vilayet">Tripolitania Vilayet</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Senussi built zawiyas, lodges that served as schools, mosques, markets and courts, among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>. Under Muhammad al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> al-Senussi the order acted as a quasi-state, settling disputes, collecting religious dues and protecting trade, while preaching a reformed, austere <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Senussi fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> from 1902 and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-libya">Italian Libya</a> from 1911; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-al-mukhtar">Omar al-Mukhtar</a> led resistance until his execution in 1931. Allied with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-of-libya">Idris al-Senussi</a> became ruler of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-cyrenaica">Emirate of Cyrenaica</a> in 1949 and king of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-libya">Kingdom of Libya</a> in 1951.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/senussi-order.jpg" alt="Senussi Order" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: BlinxTheKitty · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Senussi_Dynasty.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1843 CE – 1951 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jaghbub</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Ali al-Senussi, Muhammad al-Mahdi al-Senussi, Idris al-Senussi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Sufi)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>, <a href="/countries/chad">Chad</a>, <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Senussi, Sanusiyya, Senussi state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1897&amp;focus=senussi-order" aria-label="Senussi Order on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5318 1091 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Senussi Order lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5638 1347L5338 1239M5638 1347L5338 1239M5638 1347L5516 1265M5638 1347L5445 1677"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5338 1239h0M5338 1239h0M5516 1265h0M5445 1677h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5638 1347h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5638 1347h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jaghbub